> > an elvish leather helm (let alone another dwarven helm) then
> > fooproofing your dwarven helm is wasted.  Enchant your gloves or robe
> > or whatever instead.
>
> You ignore one situation under which things like dwarvish helms, iron
> shoes &c aren't that plentiful--if you're a lawful of a race to which
> dwarves are (or tend to be) peaceful. If you're a dwarf yourself,
> getting dwarvish armor is (ironically) rather difficult, unless you
> like annoying your god.

Just kill them.  The alignment penalty is a joke.

Even so, enchanting your gloves (or whatever) and abandoning the
dwarvish helm for an orcish helm once the former becomes very rusty is
a better use of resources than rustproofing the dwarvish helm.

> I doubt it'd ever be
> wasteful to fooproof a good bit of armor if you're using cursed scrolls
> of destroy armor (as long as it's not damaged first!).

Absolutely, I've tried to point that out throughout the thread.

> I tend to assume that anything that can be rusted will become
> rusted--thus, if I rustproof a dwarvish iron helm, in the long run I've
> effectively enchanted it twice, once I run into a rust monster.

But:
a) you're enchanting something that you're not going to be using
throughout the game (presumably you'll switch to helm of
brilliance/telepathy or elvish leather helm) and;
b) the average value of enchanting a +0, +1, or +2 piece of armor is
the same 2 points of AC, but you get them now _and_ later.  And if you
get hit by a rust monster, you can drop the helm for another (or at
least an orcish helm) and have better AC even post-attack except for
the interim period between being hit and finding another helm of any
kind.

Of course, with base AC2 items like dwarvish helms the absolute worst
case (you only get 1 point of AC with each scroll you use) is worse
than rustproofing if you happen to run into a rust monster and let it
hit your helm twice and are unable to replace the helm.  But the
average case is better even with those circumstances.  And
realistically the only long-term items with base AC2 or higher--that
is, the ones worth burning any resources on instead of focusing them on
other items that you _are_ keeping--are fooproof already (shield of
reflection, DSM) with the exception of the robe.

> And
> climbing all the way up from Gehennom to replace a &@*#$%\ helm that
> rusted is so annoying.

Unless you're doing a super-speed ascension, by gehennom you'll be in
the AC range where a point or two of AC is far from a big deal.  And
normally there are plenty of helms in gehennom anyway (by then you'll
want elvish leather over dwarvish since falling rock traps don't matter
and elvish can be enchanted higher, even neglecting spellcasting).

> Also, keep in mind that fooproofing can only be removed by some dumb
> action on the player's part--a bad run-in with a disenchanter can
> remove any evidence the thing was ever +5.

Not really, unless you get in the habit of taking off your MC3 and
meleeing it for an extended period of time.

By the time disenchanters show up, taking them down from a distance is
usually not a problem.  And if you can't, MC3 prevents disenchanting
98% of the time.

> I'd say, in the long run, fooproofing is a safer investment, *if the
> armor is something you'll keep*. For instance, fireproofing speed boots
> is probably a good investment.

Absolutely not.  For base ac:1 things (like speed boots), fooproofing
is clearly a bad idea (and is never better) until all your items are
maxed on enchantment.

For base ac:2 things, fooproofing is only usually worse, but there are
some corner-cases that _could_ make enchanting worse.  They're rare,
and except for a robe (where fireproofing it might be worthwile once
all your gear is to +3 or better) they still aren't worth fooproofing
until you've maxed enchantments on everything else--especially since
none of the damagable base AC2 items are usually long-term keepers
except the robe (an elven shield being a very unlikely but possible
alternative).



